---
import EditorialLayout from '../layouts/EditorialLayout.astro';
import Values from '../components/Values.astro';
import { site } from '../config/site';
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import danielPortrait from '../assets/about/daniel-portrait.webp';
---

<EditorialLayout
  title="About BrimFrame"
  displayFont
  eyebrow="The Solo Studio"
  description="Meet Daniel Schmid, the solo developer behind BrimFrame and Riff Racers. How indie games brought him back to gaming and where the studio name came from."
  intro={site.studioIntroduction}
>
  <section class="editorial-split">
    <h2>Who I am</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <Picture
        src={danielPortrait}
        width={1024}
        widths={[640, 1024, 1440]}
        sizes="(min-width: 52rem) 42rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
        alt="Daniel at his laptop, working on a game, on a sunlit terrace"
        formats={['avif', 'webp']}
        fallbackFormat="jpg"
        quality={90}
        class="about-portrait"
        loading="eager"
        decoding="async"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
      <p>
        I’m Daniel. I’ve worked as a web developer for about twenty years, and I
        live in Munich with my girlfriend and our cat.
      </p>
      <p>
        I played a lot of games when I was younger. Later, they took a back seat
        for a long time. Indie games got me interested again, and I started
        wanting to make something of my own.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’d like to make games for people with different tastes. I’m starting
        with a racer, but I don’t expect every game I make to be for the same
        audience.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="editorial-split">
    <h2>A solo studio</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        BrimFrame is the name I make games under. I work on my own, and Riff
        Racers is my first project here.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="editorial-split">
    <h2>Where BrimFrame came from</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I wanted a studio name I could use beyond Riff Racers. That ruled out
        anything too closely tied to racing, metal or music.
      </p>
      <p>
        I worked through a long list of names. Some were already used by other
        studios or companies. Others felt too generic. BrimFrame stuck.
      </p>
      <p>
        “Frame” made me think of the way a game shows you a world and sets its
        rules. It also has a straightforward link to games and visual media.
      </p>
      <p>
        I liked “Brim” for its sense of an edge, and for “full to the brim”:
        having more ideas than I can use at once. Together, BrimFrame felt like
        a name I could keep using as the games changed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="editorial-split">
    <h2>
      Current focus:
      <br />
      Riff Racers
    </h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Right now, most of my development time goes into Riff Racers. I’m
        working towards a first demo, with plenty left to do on the racing and
        computer-controlled drivers.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/games/">
        About Riff Racers <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="editorial-split">
    <h2>How I approach games</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Working solo means deciding what I can reasonably finish. Sometimes that
        means leaving a feature out, even when I like the idea.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="editorial-values">
    <p class="eyebrow">Values</p>
    <h2>What matters to me</h2>
    <Values />
  </section>
</EditorialLayout>
